Refill the sink's buffer after the PCM paused within a running stream (Y2Linux).

A stream that stays started can go without data for a while: Reborn drops the
PCM between tracks (~50 ms per skip), and a pause keeps the stream started
(--keep-alive=900, no AVDTP Suspend). The sink plays that time out of its
buffer, and the encoders, re-anchored at exactly real time when the PCM
resumes, never send it back. AirPods Pro (which play from their buffer, not by
RTP timestamp: patch 0007 alone did not help) crackled after two skips and went
silent after three on the developer's player (2026-11-04); the first stream
after a connect always played, since an AVDTP Start lets the sink buffer anew.

When the rate sync re-anchors in a stream that synced before, the new reference
starts as far back as the PCM was idle, at most 200 ms: the first frames go out
faster than real time and give the sink its buffer back. A new stream starts as
before. Host test with shared/rt.c: 0.5 s of audio took 0.441 s after a 50 ms
gap and 0.291 s after a 2 s pause (capped), 0.981 s for 1.0 s on a new stream.

--- a/src/io.c
+++ b/src/io.c
@@ -313,6 +313,10 @@
 	return len;
 }
 
+/* Y2: how much a resumed stream may send ahead of real time to refill the
+ * sink's buffer after a gap (io_poll_and_read_pcm). */
+#define Y2_REFILL_MS 200
+
 /**
  * Poll and read data from the PCM FIFO.
  *
@@ -418,8 +422,33 @@
 	 * there might be no data for a long time - until client starts playback.
 	 * In order to correctly calculate time drift, the zero time point has to
 	 * be obtained after the stream has started. */
-	if (io->asrs.frames == 0)
+	if (io->asrs.frames == 0) {
+		/* Y2: the stream may have run on without data for a while: a drop
+		 * between tracks (~50 ms) or a pause while it stays started
+		 * (--keep-alive). The sink played that time out of its buffer and,
+		 * paced at exactly real time from here, never gets it back: AirPods
+		 * Pro crackled after two skips and went silent after three (Y2Linux
+		 * owner's player, 2026-11-04). Start the new reference that much
+		 * earlier (at most Y2_REFILL_MS), so the first frames go out faster
+		 * than real time and refill it. A new stream (no earlier sync) starts
+		 * as before: the sink buffers anew after an AVDTP Start. */
+		const struct timespec last = io->asrs.ts;
+		const bool resumed = last.tv_sec != 0 || last.tv_nsec != 0;
 		asrsync_init(&io->asrs, pcm->rate);
+		if (resumed) {
+			struct timespec gap;
+			timespecsub(&io->asrs.ts0, &last, &gap);
+			int64_t gap_ns = (int64_t)gap.tv_sec * 1000000000 + gap.tv_nsec;
+			if (gap_ns > (int64_t)Y2_REFILL_MS * 1000000)
+				gap_ns = (int64_t)Y2_REFILL_MS * 1000000;
+			if (gap_ns > 0) {
+				const struct timespec back = {
+					.tv_sec = gap_ns / 1000000000, .tv_nsec = gap_ns % 1000000000 };
+				timespecsub(&io->asrs.ts0, &back, &io->asrs.ts0);
+				debug("PCM resumed after a gap: refilling %lld ms", (long long)(gap_ns / 1000000));
+			}
+		}
+	}
 
 	/* Mark the IO as tainted, so in case of a drain operation we will
 	 * flush any remaining frames in the encoder buffers to BT. */
